2186146822 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434327 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186146978 1078 #edb/ref 17592186146981 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186147376 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0.$ Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = t$\n\n- $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = 0$" 13194139534729 17592186045417 false] [17592186147376 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0.$ Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\nI. $y''+y'+3y = t$\n\nII. $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n\nIII. $y''+y'+3y = 0$" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186147378 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 50t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n\n- $y = R e ^ {- t} \\cos \\left( 2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n\n- $y =e^{-t}\\left( A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139534729 17592186045417 false] [17592186147378 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 50t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n\nII. $y = R e ^ {- t} \\cos \\left( 2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n\nIII. $y =e^{-t}\\left( A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186147448 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434366 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186147449 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434369 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186147450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434367 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186147450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434368 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186147451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434365 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186147451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434370 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186147705 1050 960.0 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148168 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=10{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+{e}^{-2t}$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534729 17592186045417 false] [17592186148168 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=10{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+{e}^{-2t}$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148168 1074 "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139534729 17592186045417 false] [17592186148168 1074 "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148170 1071 "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=14\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=6\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148170 1071 "A particle of mass $2kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 14kg/s^{2}$. The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 6kg/s$, and an external force given by $F(t) = 8\\cos (t)N$.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139534729 17592186045417 false] [17592186148170 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}-14y+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146798 13194139534729 17592186045417 false] [17592186148178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148178 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?" 13194139534729 17592186045417 false] [17592186148178 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148178 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y={e}^{2t}.\n$$" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148204 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=17\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{4t}+17\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+17\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148204 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 25y = 17\\sin t$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = e^{-3t}(A\\cos 4t + B\\sin 4t) - \\frac{1}{6}\\cos t + \\frac{2}{3}\\sin t$\n2. $y = Ae^{-3t} + Be^{4t} + 17\\cos t + 17\\sin t$\n3. $y = Re^{-3t}\\sin (4t + ϕ) - \\frac{1}{6}\\cos t + \\frac{2}{3}\\sin t$\n\nNote that $A$, $B$, $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534729 17592186045417 false] [17592186148204 1074 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{2}{3}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186148767 1064 "Given a forced harmonic motion equation *without* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nthe complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nAlternatively, given a forced harmonic motion equation *with* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nif the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = p \\pm \\textrm{i}q,$ then the complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y_c = R e^{p t} \\cos{(q t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y_c = R e^{p t} \\sin{(q t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y_c =e^{p t} \\left( A\\cos{q t} + B\\sin{q t} \\right)$" 13194139534729 17592186045417 false] [17592186148767 1064 "Given a forced harmonic motion equation *without* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nthe complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nAlternatively, given a forced harmonic motion equation *with* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nif the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = p \\pm \\textrm{i}q,$ then the complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R e^{p t} \\cos{(q t + 