0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =-3,$ $\\beta =5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-3+5t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5t-3.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=3,\\,Q=4.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-3t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5t-3.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-3\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )+5\\pi -3 \\\\  & =(-3{e}^{-3\\pi }+5\\pi -3)\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434364 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434366 1069 "q-94227" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434366 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434366 1072 #uuid "19aa360c-f2cb-544d-9a0e-8248f7db7158" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434366 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y={t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434360 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434367 1069 "q-94384" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434367 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+18y=45\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nII. $y=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{3t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nIII. $y={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434367 1072 #uuid "44316634-3540-5552-a378-8f950333c8f6" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434367 1074 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+18y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +18 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =2.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t, \\\\ y(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434359 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434368 1069 "q-79579" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434368 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=25t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+5t-2$\n\nII. $y=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+5t-2$\n\nIII. $y={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+t-5$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434368 1072 #uuid "4f73fcd2-bb5e-5500-9c9a-4e8cac71bdbd" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434368 1074 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a linear function, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =5$ and $\\beta =-2.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=5t-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+5t-2, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+5t-2, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+5t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly I matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434361 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434369 1069 "q-64598" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434369 1071 "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=14\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434369 1072 #uuid "6dc6dadb-3d80-5cdc-9a3e-a6f7a7c19c11" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434369 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-14y+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434363 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434370 1069 "q-94375" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434370 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=10{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $4\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, find the particle's position after $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ seconds." 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434370 1072 #uuid "b338c37e-da00-57a7-9b28-e8f59a2d36f9" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true] [17592186434370 1074 "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is an exponential force. The constant factor in the exponent is $1,$ which is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $4\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=-2,\\,Q=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-2{e}^{-\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2{e}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-\\pi /3}+2{e}^{\\pi /3})\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534729 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0246830e-5242-506a-a36d-7e18041dc699" 17592186434319 "answer-0cf08c7c-2d36-50bb-9e3b-685c8d932615" 17592186434320 "answer-0f41aab3-f2f4-5bf7-976e-0433ba6153b4" 17592186434321 "answer-10d1ddb0-54aa-5cff-b655-e585e1133a03" 17592186434322 "answer-10d59970-014a-5390-ad87-b6c264b278a7" 17592186434323 "answer-1a9369f8-1713-511b-9b87-3dc374534713" 17592186434324 "answer-22e73a05-a802-5a24-84ff-9321845a62ef" 17592186434325 "answer-28c9a6d3-48f4-5ead-a3e4-26a22313a88d" 17592186434326 "answer-2bdc58f4-a0f5-5ee9-b39e-8ca44ac7e1aa" 17592186434327 "answer-2e435eba-7562-5fb0-810c-8dac762b4077" 17592186434328 "answer-3a4b5dac-8848-5c3d-8255-14ec9a67a346" 17592186434329 "answer-417d10b3-1087-524f-b6b3-6b1ff74203e3" 17592186434330 "answer-4a146e1f-9b71-5c45-9fa4-2c70c38fdb05" 17592186434331 "answer-56209a80-3fbc-5c41-befa-82b2a27f33a5" 17592186434332 "answer-60d46327-b6cd-5b92-bdea-875e3d390e6b" 17592186434333 "answer-61746440-d9e6-5045-bb93-e252521a61b2" 17592186434334 "answer-66babac9-7d04-5021-9b7c-f7e425d4dffe" 17592186434335 "answer-6792e6ec-4ba8-544e-9f7b-9648a4712648" 17592186434336 "answer-71438c51-3e77-5b6b-8ede-3b96533c54f5" 17592186434337 "answer-74a3f2b4-0ca6-5951-a6dd-e2cbdba2de6f" 17592186434338 "answer-897e1658-e2d8-558a-a228-a8db420f366d" 17592186434339 "answer-8fca051f-7e60-5e0c-902f-804346bada68" 17592186434340 "answer-98ffd1e3-f246-5eda-a2b1-a417f1090519" 17592186434341 "answer-9d8d329c-068a-50cc-b2fb-4a2c309c7ede" 17592186434342 "answer-a16996e7-7d46-5977-a350-af7a4818e178" 17592186434343 "answer-ac0f1868-76f6-576c-a678-b5319b6fe27d" 17592186434344 "answer-adc1bc4b-3da5-5003-87de-2b882c60ff13" 17592186434345 "answer-b409ad64-6d88-5925-9ac6-e8d1f16d87e0" 17592186434346 "answer-c54d927c-6507-5c2b-ae0d-0a803a216556" 17592186434347 "answer-cd2e5274-4651-5496-821c-a506d7dde348" 1759218643